  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure is sliding southeast across the region\nthis afternoon, which has brought light winds and sunshine for \na large part of the area. Temperatures have risen nicely into \nthe 40s for most, with highs expected to reach the mid to upper \n40s. This is a few degrees warmer than guidance, serving as an \nexcellent illustration of the power of even early November \nsunshine on dark ground. As we head into tonight, the main \nforecast concern is the development of fog and stratus. There is\na layer of stratus lingering over eastern North Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota this afternoon. The warming temperatures and \nmixing of the boundary layer are quickly eating away at the \nedges of that stratus, but it is a rather large area for it to \ncompletely clear this afternoon before we lose the sunshine. \n\nSoundings from this morning at Aberdeen and Bismarck put \nmoisture associated with this stratus predominantly below 925mb.\nWherever it ends up by late this afternoon, it is expected to \nexpand once again after sunset, even as it advects northeast in \nthe low-level flow. The heavy rainfall from earlier this week \nhas also contributed a lot of moisture to the boundary layer, \nhelping keep dewpoints in the 20s to low 30s over our area. The \nbig question is whether the stratus from the west will expand \ninto the area before or after fog forms, and what the intensity \nof either will be. Model guidance is struggling with the very \nshallow nature of the moisture, with some models going straight \nto stratus, and others giving us fog. For now I have favored \nkeeping the stratus farther west with more fog development and \ncolder temperatures. If that stratus layer expands and spreads \neast more quickly than anticipated, it will keep temperatures \nfrom dropping off as much as we have currently forecast and \nthere will be less fog. \n\nWhatever fog and stratus materializes tonight will gradually \nmix out Saturday morning, making way for another mostly sunny \nand mild day. Southwest winds will increase through the \nafternoon, bringing breezy conditions with gusts pushing toward \n20 to 25 mph. This will help drive temperatures warmer than \ntoday, with upper 40s and lower 50s expected. A weak and \nmoisture-starved cold front will cross the area late Saturday \nnight into Sunday, shifting our winds to the west and northwest \nfor Sunday. Despite this frontal passage, temperatures will \nremain above normal to close out the weekend.\n\nA remarkably dry and quiet pattern will remain locked in place \nacross the region for Monday through Thursday. Global models are\nkeeping the primary storm track well north of the International\nBorder. While a few weak shortwaves may pass near the area \nearly in the week, precipitation chances remain low as the \nbetter dynamics and moisture will be confined to Ontario and \nManitoba. High temperatures will consistently reach the mid 40s \nto mid 50s, which is notably above our normal late autumn \naverages. Overnight lows will still drop below freezing for most\nlocations, but the overall weather pattern will feel \nexceptionally mild and tranquil.",
